digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper motivates developing and evaluating an improved shallow quantum-metrology protocol for gate calibration that addresses phase-matching constraints, CRB/coefficient-optimal performance, and realistic drift/coherence limitations in order to close the precision gap relevant to high-accuracy quantum gate characterization.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Accuracy gaps, phase-matching failures, mainstream-protocol limitations, and realistic drift/coherence constraints jointly motivate protocol designs that are more practical and metrologically optimal for gate calibration."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) The observed gap between common gate-angle estimation accuracy and the accuracy needed for QEC threshold verification motivates new metrology methods to close that gap."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because realistic constraints make the scaling coefficient a key challenge, practical metrology protocol design should focus on coefficient-optimal and CRB-oriented performance rather than Heisenberg scaling alone."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) RPE is a main gate-calibration approach whose precision degrades under time-dependent drifts common in superconducting qubits."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) RB is a main gate-calibration approach, but it forgoes Heisenberg scaling."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The accuracy gap between common gate-angle estimation protocols and QEC-level characterization needs supports seeking improved metrology beyond current accuracy levels."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) A research direction is to investigate shallow, drift-robust gate-calibration protocols as a way to mitigate finite-coherence, drift, and deep-circuit limitations."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) RB is one main gate-calibration approach with practical limitations, including extensive circuit depth requirements and insensitivity to coherent rotation errors."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The observed failure of multi-parameter RPE to reach Heisenberg scaling or the classical CRB motivates investigating an additional structural condition, such as phase matching, rather than treating it as already proven by deduction."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) For gate-calibration metrology, seeking overall optimality motivates designs that jointly improve coefficient or CRB-oriented performance and the quantum circuit component."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) In realistic devices with finite coherence and time-dependent drift, protocols requiring deep circuits have limited achievable precision and practicality."];
  "i1" [label="(0,0,0) If a task requires accuracy ~1e-4 rad but available methods deliver only ~1e-2–1e-3 rad, then new metrology methods are needed to close the accuracy gap."];
  "i15" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that violating phase-matching causes failure of Heisenberg/CRB, and that multi-parameter RPE relies on phase-matching, therefore phase-matching is a key practical constraint to address in protocol design."];
  "i19" [label="(0,0,0) If metrology efficiency depends on both Heisenberg scaling and the prefactor, and the key challenge is prefactor optimality under realistic constraints, then practical protocol design should focus on coefficient-optimal and CRB-oriented performance."];
  "i2" [label="(0,0,0) If a protocol requires deep circuits, then in realistic devices (finite coherence + drift) its achievable precision and practicality will be limited."];
  "i5" [label="(0,0,0) Multi-parameter RPE sometimes does not reach Heisenberg scaling / classical CRB in practice even without quantum noise (a mismatch with ideal expectations)."];
  "i6" [label="(0,0,0) Hidden identifiability/structural conditions in multi-parameter phase estimation can prevent reaching Heisenberg scaling and classical CRB unless satisfied."];
  "i7" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the observed failure of multi-parameter RPE to reach Heisenberg/CRB, and knowing structural conditions can cause such failures, therefore hypothesize an additional necessary condition (e.g., phase matching of diagonal elements)."];
  "i8" [label="(0,0,0) If violating the phase-matching condition implies failure of Heisenberg scaling and classical CRB for multi-parameter RPE, then diagnosing/enforcing or bypassing this condition is necessary for practical optimal metrology."];
  "i9" [label="(0,0,0) If a protocol is robust to time-dependent errors and uses shallow circuits, then it mitigates drift/coherence limitations and reduces the need for deep-circuit overhead."];
  "n10" [label="(10,0,0) With time-dependent drifts (common in superconducting qubits), RPE precision degrades to ~1e-1 to 1e-2 rad."];
  "n12" [label="(12,0,0) Randomized benchmarking is general but it forgoes Heisenberg scaling."];
  "n13" [label="(13,0,0) RB requires extensive circuit depth for accurate estimation and is insensitive to coherent rotation errors."];
  "n18" [label="(18,0,0) Achieving overall optimality requires improving both classical post-processing and quantum circuit design in gate calibration."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Many metrology strategies achieve Heisenberg scaling, but with realistic constraints the key challenge is making the scaling coefficient (prefactor) optimal."];
  "n20" [label="(20,0,0) If phase-matching is violated, then multi-parameter RPE fails to achieve Heisenberg scaling and also fails to meet the classical Cramér–Rao bound (even without quantum noise)."];
  "n4" [label="(6,0,0) Gate-angle estimation accuracy of common protocols is only ~1e-2 to 1e-3 rad, but QEC threshold verification needs ~1e-4 rad; thus there is an accuracy gap."];
  "n6" [label="(5,0,0) Finite coherence times and time-dependent drift from low-frequency noise/control fluctuations further compound metrology inefficiency."];
  "n7" [label="(7,0,0) Gate-calibration metrology mainly uses two approaches: robust phase estimation (RPE) and randomized benchmarking (RB)."];
  "i6" -> "i7" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "i5" -> "i7" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "i8" -> "i15" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n20" -> "i15" [label="deduction-case"];
